  "text": "是多少?去一步一步详细计算. Here’s the step-by-step calculation to find the radius of the cylinder’s base:\n\n---\n\n### **Given:**\n1. The wire makes an angle of \\(60^\\circ\\) with the horizontal plane.\n2. A sphere is placed 10 cm away from the point where the wire touches the plane.\n3. The radius of the sphere (\\(r_{\\text{sphere}}\\)) is 2 cm.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 1: Understand the geometry**\n- The wire touches the horizontal plane at a point on the cylinder’s side.\n- The sphere is placed 10 cm away from this point, which means the spherical cap (the part of the sphere touching the wire) has a height of 10 cm.\n- The wire is tangent to the sphere at the point where it touches the cylinder.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 2: Analyze the spherical cap**\nFor a sphere of radius \\(r = 2\\) cm, the space from the center to the edge of the sphere is 2 cm. When a spherical cap is cut from the sphere, the height of the cap (\\(h\\)) is given by:\n\\[\nh = \\text{radius} - \\text{distance from center},\n\\]\nwhere the distance from the center is equal to \\(r_{\\text{cylinder}}\\) (the radius of the cylinder’s base), and \\(r = 2\\) cm. Thus:\n\\[\nh = 2 - r_{\\text{cylinder}}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### **Step 3: Use trigonometry to relate the height of the cap to the angle**\nThe wire makes an angle of \\(60^\\circ\\) with the horizontal plane. This means the wire touches the horizontal plane at a point that is \\(r_{\\text{cylinder}}\\) units away from the top of the cylinder and at a height of \\(h = 2 - r_{\\text{cylinder}}\\).\n\nUsing the tangent of the angle:\n\\[\n\\tan(60^\\circ) = \\frac{h}{r_{\\text{cylinder}}},\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\tan(60^\\circ) = \\sqrt{3}\\), so:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{3} = \\frac{2 - r_{\\text{cylinder}}}{r_{\\text{cylinder}}}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### **Step 4: Solve for \\(r_{\\text{cylinder}}\\)**\nMultiply both sides by \\(r_{\\text{cylinder}}\\):\n\\[\n\\sqrt{3} \\cdot r_{\\text{cylinder}} = 2 - r_{\\text{cylinder}}.\n\\]\nBring all terms involving \\(r_{\\text{cylinder}}\\) to one side:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{3} \\cdot r_{\\text{cylinder}} + r_{\\text{cylinder}} = 2.\n\\]\nFactor out \\(r_{\\text{cylinder}}\\):\n\\[\nr_{\\text{cylinder}} (\\sqrt{3} + 1) = 2.\n\\]\nDivide both sides by \\(\\sqrt{3} + 1\\):\n\\[\nr_{\\text{cylinder}} = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3} + 1}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### **Step 5: Rationalize the denominator**\nMultiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator (\\(\\sqrt{3} - 1\\)):\n\\[\nr_{\\text{cylinder}} = \\frac{2 (\\sqrt{3} - 1)}{ (\\sqrt{3} + 1)(\\sqrt{3} - 1)}.\n\\]\nSimplify the denominator:\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{3} + 1)(\\sqrt{3} - 1) = 3 - 1 = 2.\n\\]\nSo:\n\\[\nr_{\\text{cylinder}} = \\frac{2 (\\sqrt{3} - 1)}{2} = \\sqrt{3} - 1.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### **Final Answer:**\nThe radius of the cylinder’s base is \\(\\boxed{\\sqrt{3} - 1}\\).",
